clouds of steam covered the earth  near the city of goma after a long night  of fear  and uncertainty a volcano beneath mount  neregongo  expelled streams of lava from its crater  and these currents had flowed to the  city's outskirts  near the border with neighboring rwanda  hundreds of homes were buried or burnt  but the mountains seemed to settle in  the early morning light  we're still waiting as we can see it's  still smoking  we think the lava is heading towards  rwanda but i don't think it's going to  get that far  god willing it will stop right here  there was no warning given  of this eruption the night sky pulsing  red  as molten rock swept down the slopes of  mount neregongo  when a sea of lava built down below the  local government gave the order to  evacuate  but many had already made that decision  for themselves  some crossed the border into rwanda  others boarded boats  on the nearby lake the residents of  gomer  have begun to assess the damage a river  of rock  now squats on the city's edge the rubble  some two or three stories high in places  tangled remains of homes and businesses  are visible  in places and residents will need months  or years  to rebuild their lives they left a home  and they're coming back there is  nothing the house is not there all their  belongings their life investment is not  there  and people are crushed so that's that's  the situation right now that people are  coming back but they really  need uh support they need urgent help  because they've come back to to nothing  the volcano has cooled  and scientists say the city has been  fortunate  but that's little comfort to hundreds of  families who've lost  their homes see you and then  now i don't know where to go and we're  afraid because the fire is still visible  in the lava  life in gomer has never been easy but  the angry mountain  has made it worse john sparks sky news
 

